NNAAMMEE
       faillog - display faillog records or set login failure limits

SSYYNNOOPPSSIISS
       ffaaiilllloogg [_o_p_t_i_o_n_s]

DDEESSCCRRIIPPTTIIOONN
       ffaaiilllloogg displays the contents of the failure log database
       (/var/log/faillog). It can also set the failure counters and limits.
       When ffaaiilllloogg is run without arguments, it only displays the faillog
       records of the users who had a login failure.

OOPPTTIIOONNSS
       The options which apply to the ffaaiilllloogg command are:

       --aa, ----aallll
           Display (or act on) faillog records for all users having an entry
           in the faillog database.

           The range of users can be restricted with the --uu option.

           In display mode, this is still restricted to existing users but
           forces the display of the faillog entries even if they are empty.

           With the --ll, --mm, --rr, --tt options, the users' records are changed,
           even if the user does not exist on the system. This is useful to
           reset records of users that have been deleted or to set a policy in
           advance for a range of users.

       --hh, ----hheellpp
           Display help message and exit.

       --ll, ----lloocckk--sseeccss _S_E_C
           Lock account for _S_E_C seconds after failed login.

           Write access to /var/log/faillog is required for this option.

       --mm, ----mmaaxxiimmuumm _M_A_X
           Set the maximum number of login failures after the account is
           disabled to _M_A_X.

           Selecting a _M_A_X value of 0 has the effect of not placing a limit on
           the number of failed logins.

           The maximum failure count should always be 0 for _r_o_o_t to prevent a
           denial of services attack against the system.

           Write access to /var/log/faillog is required for this option.

       --rr, ----rreesseett
           Reset the counters of login failures.

           Write access to /var/log/faillog is required for this option.

       --RR, ----rroooott _C_H_R_O_O_T___D_I_R
           Apply changes in the _C_H_R_O_O_T___D_I_R directory and use the configuration
           files from the _C_H_R_O_O_T___D_I_R directory. Only absolute paths are
           supported.

       --tt, ----ttiimmee _D_A_Y_S
           Display faillog records more recent than _D_A_Y_S.

       --uu, ----uusseerr _L_O_G_I_N|_R_A_N_G_E
           Display faillog record or maintains failure counters and limits (if
           used with --ll, --mm or --rr options) only for the specified user(s).

           The users can be specified by a login name, a numerical user ID, or
           a _R_A_N_G_E of users. This _R_A_N_G_E of users can be specified with a min
           and max values (_U_I_D___M_I_N_-_U_I_D___M_A_X), a max value (_-_U_I_D___M_A_X), or a min
           value (_U_I_D___M_I_N_-).

       When none of the --ll, --mm, or --rr options are used, ffaaiilllloogg displays the
       faillog record of the specified user(s).

CCAAVVEEAATTSS
       ffaaiilllloogg only prints out users with no successful login since the last
       failure. To print out a user who has had a successful login since their
       last failure, you must explicitly request the user with the --uu flag, or
       print out all users with the --aa flag.

FFIILLEESS
       /var/log/faillog
